Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.50,0:00:05.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}How do you pick the right attention mechanism for a long-range Transformer task,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.60,0:00:08.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and could mixing several types work even better?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.65,0:00:10.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This paper, DARTFormer,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.12,0:00:14.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}adapts differentiable neural architecture search to answer both questions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:14.83,0:00:20.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The authors build a supernetwork that contains many efficient attention types in parallel,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.57,0:00:24.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}then use a masked validation accuracy drop metric to rank them.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.58,0:00:28.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Their search reliably finds the best single attention for a task,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.72,0:00:34.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}but a surprising second result emerges: heterogeneous Transformers that combine several
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.26,0:00:37.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}attention types beat the average homogeneous model,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:37.51,0:00:39.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}yet never beat the single best one.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.04,0:00:45.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The Transformer's original dot-product attention scales quadratically with sequence length,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.48,0:00:48.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so a large family of efficient alternatives has appeared,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.88,0:00:52.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}from Longformer and Bigbird to Performer, Reformer, and Synthesizer.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.95,0:00:58.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The catch is that no single one wins everywhere: earlier long-range benchmarking showed
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.14,0:01:02.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}that the best attention depends heavily on the task when there is no pretraining.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.98,0:01:05.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}That leaves practitioners with an awkward question,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:06.03,0:01:11.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}namely how to efficiently discover which attention is right for a given long-range task
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.23,0:01:13.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}without simply training all of them.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.68,0:01:16.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}There is a long-standing intuition that each attention head can specialize,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:17.05,0:01:20.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}learning a different kind of relationship,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.41,0:01:23.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}much as convolutional kernels learn different features.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.78,0:01:27.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}If that is true,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.14,0:01:30.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}then a Transformer mixing several attention types could learn a richer set of relationships
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.51,0:01:33.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and outperform any single-attention model.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.87,0:01:37.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This paper takes that intuition seriously and asks directly whether the optimal attention
Dialogue: 0,0:01:37.24,0:01:40.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}for a task is actually a mixture of different attentions,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.60,0:01:43.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}rather than assuming the answer.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.27,0:01:48.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper makes three contributions.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.18,0:01:52.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First, it proposes DARTFormer,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.09,0:01:55.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a differentiable-architecture-search-style method that efficiently finds the best attention for a task.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.00,0:01:59.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:59.91,0:02:03.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it extends that framework to build and search for heterogeneous Transformers that combine multiple attention types,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.82,0:02:07.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}using two procedures: an expensive iterative pruning method they call NAS Prune,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.73,0:02:11.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and a cheap single-pass method called NAS One-shot.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.64,0:02:15.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Third, and most importantly,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.55,0:02:19.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it shows empirically that these heterogeneous Transformers cannot outperform the best
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.46,0:02:23.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}homogeneous Transformer on the long-range NLP tasks studied.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:23.67,0:02:29.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DARTFormer builds a single-layer supernetwork that holds one candidate block for every
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.09,0:02:31.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}efficient attention type in parallel.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.42,0:02:35.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Borrowing the fixed-alpha trick from differentiable architecture search,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.96,0:02:40.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it does not learn softmax edge weights and skips bi-level optimization;
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.44,0:02:43.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}instead it simply averages the candidate block outputs,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.91,0:02:47.67,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which lets the supernetwork train all the way to convergence.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.75,0:02:49.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}To rank the attentions,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.20,0:02:53.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it uses a masked validation accuracy drop metric: mask out one block,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:53.55,0:02:56.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}measure how far validation accuracy falls,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.20,0:03:00.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the block whose removal hurts the most is judged the best.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.11,0:03:05.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For homogeneous models the top-scoring block is chosen; for heterogeneous ones,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.09,0:03:08.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}blocks are either pruned worst-first with fine-tuning,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.49,0:03:12.89,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}or the top four are taken directly and stacked into a full Transformer.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:13.27,0:03:18.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}All experiments run on three tasks drawn from the Long Range Arena benchmark,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.36,0:03:20.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}chosen to stress long-sequence modeling.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.01,0:03:27.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}These are byte-level binary text classification on IMDb with sequences of one thousand tokens,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:27.22,0:03:31.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}ListOps ten-way classification with two-thousand-token sequences,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.52,0:03:35.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and byte-level document matching with four-thousand-token sequences.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.02,0:03:40.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The search considers nine candidate attention mechanisms, including Bigbird,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.05,0:03:46.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Linear Transformer, Linformer, Local attention, Longformer, Performer, Reformer,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:46.34,0:03:48.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Sparse Transformer, and Synthesizer,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.72,0:03:53.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}with hyperparameters largely matching the original Long Range Arena setup.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.91,0:03:55.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The headline finding splits in two.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.07,0:03:57.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On the positive side,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.37,0:04:02.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the masked-validation-drop search is genuinely effective at homogeneous selection: it
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.61,0:04:07.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}correctly identifies the best attention for text classification and for ListOps,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.55,0:04:14.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}where Reformer stands out with a drop score of nearly twelve while every other attention scores below half a point.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.65,0:04:15.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On the negative side,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.94,0:04:21.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}when the authors assemble heterogeneous Transformers that mix several attention types,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:21.25,0:04:25.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}none of them beats the best single-attention model on any of the three tasks.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.00,0:04:30.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Mixing attention helps you beat the average choice, but not the best one.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.81,0:04:36.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}A useful practical takeaway comes from comparing the two heterogeneous search procedures.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:36.23,0:04:38.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The expensive NAS Prune method,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.12,0:04:41.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which repeatedly removes the worst block and fine-tunes,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.53,0:04:46.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}offers no consistent advantage over the far cheaper NAS One-shot method that just takes
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.83,0:04:49.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the top-scoring attentions in a single pass,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:49.51,0:04:52.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}at least when good attentions are correctly identified.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.86,0:04:58.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Attempts to tilt the mixture toward the strongest attentions or give them more heads also
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.28,0:05:00.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}failed to produce consistent gains,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.42,0:05:03.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reinforcing that the cheap method is good enough.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:03.70,0:05:06.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}To put concrete numbers on it: on ListOps,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.44,0:05:11.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Reformer earns a masked-validation-drop score of eleven point eight five,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.19,0:05:15.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}dwarfing every other attention's score of under half a point,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:15.16,0:05:17.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a strikingly clean selection signal.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:17.51,0:05:21.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On document matching, the best homogeneous model, Synthesizer,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.55,0:05:23.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reaches seventy-one point one percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.02,0:05:31.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}while the heterogeneous NAS Prune and NAS One-shot models trail at sixty-seven and sixty-four point seven percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.45,0:05:37.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On text classification the best homogeneous Performer hits sixty-four point five percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:37.24,0:05:40.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and again neither heterogeneous variant edges ahead.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:40.63,0:05:45.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Across nine attention mechanisms and three tasks, the pattern is consistent.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:45.88,0:05:49.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For a given task it is often unclear which attention will do best,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.00,0:05:51.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and DARTFormer offers a cheap,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:51.87,0:05:57.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reliable recipe: train a single-layer mixed-attention Transformer and use masked validation
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.55,0:05:59.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}accuracy drop to pick the winner,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:59.61,0:06:05.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}keeping in mind that very low drop scores are a warning that the choice may be unreliable.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:05.23,0:06:08.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The deeper lesson is a cautionary one for the field.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.48,0:06:13.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Combining diverse attention mechanisms does beat the average single choice,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:13.16,0:06:15.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}but it never beats the best single choice,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.78,0:06:22.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which suggests the different attentions do not simply add complementary biases the way common intuition assumes.
